{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "21:A fundamental contribution to deep learning that underpins many modern trustworthy approaches is the development of deep residual learning frameworks. The seminal work on **Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** (arXiv: 1512.03385) introduced residual connections that enabled the training of substantially deeper neural networks. By reformulating layers to learn residual functions rather than direct mappings, this work addressed the degradation problem that previously limited network depth. While primarily focused on performance, the architectural innovations introduced in this work have implications for model robustness and reliability, as deeper representations can capture more nuanced features and potentially improve generalization under distribution shifts.\n25:The emergence of large generative language models has introduced new trustworthiness challenges. Recent comprehensive evaluations have sought to systematically assess the reliability, safety, and potential risks of these powerful systems. Notably, **DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** (arXiv: 2306.11698) provides an extensive evaluation framework for analyzing GPT models across multiple trustworthiness dimensions including harmful content generation, toxicity, bias, factuality, and robustness. This work establishes benchmark datasets and evaluation protocols that have become important references for the community studying the trustworthiness of generative AI systems. The findings from this assessment highlight the need for continued research into alignment techniques, constitutional AI approaches, and robust evaluation methodologies to ensure these models operate safely in real-world applications.\n29:As AI systems become more autonomous and capable, there is growing interest in developing automated methods for identifying vulnerabilities and security risks. The field of red-teaming—systematically attempting to elicit undesirable behaviors from AI systems—has emerged as a critical component of trustworthiness research. Recent work on **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** (arXiv: 2510.02609) addresses the specific challenges of evaluating code-generating AI agents. This work introduces automated methodologies for probing code agents for vulnerabilities, unsafe behavior, and security flaws through systematic red-teaming. The approaches developed in this work are relevant to the broader trustworthy machine learning community, as they demonstrate how automated evaluation can scale to test diverse AI capabilities and identify weaknesses before deployment.\n65:[1] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv: 1512.03385, 2015.\n67:[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv: 2306.11698, 2023.\n69:[3] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv: 2510.02609, 2025.\n",
